-- title:
--   Inertia twisted by a diamond fixes the second Igusa component
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\kappa$ denotes the residue field $\mathrm{ResidueField}(Pl)$ of a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and $X = \mathrm{TwoChartModel}\,A\,K\,j$ with structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ denotes the two-chart model built from the two subalgebras of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, resp. over $A[j^{-1}]$.
--
--   **Arithmetic and function-field data.** $Pl$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; $p$ is a prime; $M$ is a non-zero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$ (`hM`, `hpM`); $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity (`hζ`); $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((t))$ equal to the base change [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield of Laurent series generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ (`hK`); $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), together with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with that of $L$; and $j \in K$ is non-zero with Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (`hj`). The residue field $\kappa$ is assumed algebraically closed, of characteristic $p$, and an $A$-algebra.
--
--   **The two components of the special fibre.** $C_1, C_2$ are schemes with proper, geometrically integral, smooth of relative dimension $1$ morphisms $c_1, c_2$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$, equipped with closed immersions $i_1, i_2$ into the base change $X_\kappa$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$; `hcover` says that every point of $X_\kappa$ lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says the scheme-theoretic intersection $\mathrm{pullback}(i_1,i_2)$ is reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` say that its cardinality is $n$ with $0 < n$. The section $\varepsilon$ of $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, the sections $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ of $c_1, c_2$, and `hε₁` (the composite of $\varepsilon_1$ with $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $\kappa$) pin down the cusp and place its reduction on $C_1$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** $D$ is a relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ designation for $X \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ (a scheme $D.P$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ with a zero section), `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents, with Poincaré rigidified line bundle, the sub-Picard functor cut out by fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero, and `hsm`, `hsep` that $D.toBase$ is smooth and separated. `hreps` is the corresponding representability statement for the base change to $\kappa$, and `hPk` identifies its Poincaré bundle with the one obtained from $D$ by base change. $D_1, D_2$ are designations for $c_1, c_2$ with representability hypotheses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`. The morphism $\nu_2$ from $D_\kappa$ to $D_2$ over $\kappa$ together with `hν₂` expresses that for every $\kappa$-scheme $T$ and every point $a$ of $D_\kappa$ over $T$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and `pullback.snd c₂ t`, of the pullback along `curveChange i₂` of the bundle classified by $a$. Further geometric hypotheses: $X \to \operatorname{Spec}A$ is proper; $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is an $A$- and an $L$-algebra compatibly; `hsmL`, `hgiL` state smoothness of relative dimension $1$ and geometric integrality of $X_L \to \operatorname{Spec}L$, and `hprL`, `hgcL` properness and geometric connectedness of $D_L \to \operatorname{Spec}L$.
--
--   **Identification of the geometric fibre and its Galois action.** $M_\eta$ is a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), $e_\eta$ an isomorphism of $M_\eta.C$ with $X_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`), with the chart preimage non-empty; `hMηpin` pins the identification by $q$-expansions: every element of the finite chart algebra has, as a function on $M_\eta.C$, the Laurent expansion obtained from its expansion in $K$ by applying $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to coefficients. `hgal` states that for every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism $g$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ fixing $L$ pointwise, the bijection $M_\eta.pointEquivPlace$ between $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points and places is equivariant for the action of [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) of $g$. `hin` and `hcomm` are the Hecke/diamond input and commutativity predicates `HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)` and `HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)` at level $Mp$.
--
--   **Semilinear structures and the abstract special fibre.** A multiplicative semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ on $A$ is given, compatible with $A \to L$ (`hΓA`). $G$ is a datum `NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`: abelian groups $J0s$, $JI$, $JE$, a subgroup `torus` of $J0s$ and a surjection $\mathrm{proj} : J0s \to JI \times JE$ with kernel `torus`. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $J0s$, $JI$, $JE$ with the $\kappa$-points of $D_\kappa$, $D_1$, $D_2$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that addition corresponds to tensor product of the pulled-back Poincaré bundles; `hproj` says that the two components of $\mathrm{proj}$ correspond to composition with the pullback morphism to $D_1$ attached to $i_1$ and with $\nu_2$; `hPTS` says that `pts` is additive for the relative group law and sends $0$ to the unit.
--
--   **Hecke, diamond and Galois operators on $D$.** `gpts` is a bijection between [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero Picard group of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`, and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D$; $\varphi$ assigns to each element of the Hecke algebra `HeckeAlgOne` an endomorphism of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec}A$, and $\tau$ to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a semilinear endomorphism of $D$ over the induced map of $\operatorname{Spec}A$. The hypotheses `hφmul` (additivity of $\varphi(t)$ for the relative group law), `hφpts` (compatibility of `gpts` with the Hecke module structure `heckeModuleOneBar`), `hτ1`, `hτmul` (the $\tau$ form an action), `hτφ` (Hecke and Galois operators commute), `hgadd` (additivity of `gpts`) and `hτpts` (Galois semilinearity of `gpts`) constrain these operators.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi data.** `hDL`, `ajL`, `kL`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hPL`, `hajLε`, `hajL`, `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_aj` (summarised here) provide the Abel–Jacobi morphism of $X_L$ into $D_L$ normalised by the cusp, its characterisation by pullbacks of the Poincaré bundle as the line bundle of a point tensored with the ideal module of the cusp, its base change $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-avatar `ajbar` with the point `εbar` over the cusp, and the statement that for any two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points, one of them lying over the cusp, the class of their difference divisor is carried by `gpts` to the composite with `ajbar`.
--
--   **Operators on the abstract fibre, integral points, and the norm relation.** $O$ is a datum `NeronSpecialFibreOpsV3 G` (Hecke operators, diamond operators indexed by $(\mathbb{Z}/p)^\times$ and by integers, the Atkin–Lehner involution, an inertia action, and their compatibilities), and `hO` has four clauses: the Hecke operator $O.hecke\ \ell$ corresponds under `pts` to composition with $\varphi$ of `heckeGenOne` at $\ell$; $O.diamondP\ b$ corresponds to $\varphi(\mathrm{diamondGen}\ d)$ for every $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d \equiv 1 \bmod M$ and $d \equiv b \bmod p$; $O.diamondN\ d$ corresponds to $\varphi(\mathrm{diamondGen}\ d)$ for $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d \equiv 1 \bmod p$; and $O.inertia\ b$ corresponds to $\tau(s)$ for every $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ with $s\zeta = \zeta^{b}$. `hPl` says $p$ lies in the non-units of $Pl$, and $\rho$, `hρ`, `hAlgκ` give a ring map $A \to Pl$ inducing $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and $A \to \kappa$.
--
--   `hF3` is the $\mathcal{O}_I$-points hypothesis: for every subgroup $I$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ contained in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, acting trivially on the $p$-th roots of unity, and of finite index in that inertia subgroup, writing $\mathcal{O}_I = Pl \cap \overline{\mathbb{Q}}^{\,I}$, there exist a ring map $\rho_I : A \to \mathcal{O}_I$ inducing $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that, with $\mathrm{dom}$ the set of $x \in \mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$ whose point `gpts x` factors through an $\mathcal{O}_I$-point of $D$: $I$ fixes $\mathrm{dom}$ pointwise; such a factorisation is unique; $\mathrm{dom}$ contains $0$ and is closed under subtraction; for every $n > 0$ with $p \nmid n$, an $n$-torsion $\mathcal{O}_I$-point with trivial reduction is trivial and every $n$-torsion $\kappa$-point lifts to an $n$-torsion $\mathcal{O}_I$-point; and $\mathrm{dom}$ is stable under any Frobenius element at $Pl$ normalising $I$.
--
--   $\mathcal{A} \to \operatorname{Spec}A$ with closed immersion $\iota$ into $D$ satisfies `h𝒜`: $\iota$ is a closed immersion, $\mathcal{A} \to \operatorname{Spec}A$ is proper and smooth with connected geometric fibres, the image of $\mathcal{A}$ is closed under unit, multiplication and inverse for the relative group law, the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $\mathcal{A}$ are exactly the image under `gpts` of the subgroup [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29), and $\mathcal{A}$ is stable under every Hecke operator $\varphi(t)$. `hF10` is the norm relation: for every $\kappa$-algebra $T'$ and every $T'$-point $v$ of $D_\kappa$ that lifts to $\mathcal{A}$, composing $v$ with $\varphi$ of $\sum_{b \in (\mathbb{Z}/p)^\times} \mathrm{diamondGen}\,d_b$, where $d_b$ is the Chinese-remainder lift of $(b,1)$, gives the unit point.
--
--   **Igusa identification of the special fibre.** $w$ is an integral weight-one form of level $M$ over $\kappa$; $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ are curve models over $\kappa$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C κ M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$, $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ over $\kappa$ (`he₁`, `he₂`); the chart preimage in $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C$ is non-empty; `hgauss₁` pins the identification of $C_1$ by reduction of $q$-expansions: if an element $a$ of the finite chart algebra satisfies $a \cdot y = x$ in Laurent series over $L$ for power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ having non-zero reduction, then the corresponding function on $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C$ has Laurent expansion the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$. `hεC₂` says the reduced cusp section avoids $C_2$, and `hεgal` that $\varepsilon$ is equivariant for any endomorphism of the two-chart model induced on the finite chart algebra by a coefficientwise Galois twist.
--
--   `nodesIg` is a finite set of pairs of places of the Igusa function field, $\Psi : J0s \cong \mathrm{GluedPic}^0(\kappa, \text{Igusa}, \mathrm{nodesIg})$ and $\theta_1, \theta_2$ are isomorphisms of $JI$, $JE$ with $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field, and `hF4c` has seven clauses: `nodesIg` consists exactly of the pairs of places cut out by $\kappa$-points of the intersection of $C_1$ and $C_2$; its cardinality is $n$; both projections are injective on it; $\mathrm{toPic0Pair} \circ \Psi$ agrees with $(\theta_1, \theta_2) \circ \mathrm{proj}$; the image of `torus` under $\Psi$ is the range of `nodeUnit`; and $\theta_1$, $\theta_2$ are computed by the Abel–Jacobi recipe on $C_1$, $C_2$ relative to $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$. `frobT` with `hfrobT` is a semilinear automorphism of the Igusa function field raising every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power.
--
--   **The abstract specialisation family.** For each subgroup $I$ as in `hF3`, $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I)$ is a subgroup of $\mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$ and $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)$ an additive map from it to $\mathrm{GluedPic}^0$. `hIFm` is the two-line interface: membership in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I)$ is equivalent to the point `gpts y` factoring through $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal{O}_I$ via the map chosen by `hF3`; and for such a factorisation, $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)$ of $y$ equals $\Psi$ applied to the $\kappa$-point obtained by reduction. `hlev` has two clauses: for $J \le I$ both admissible, any $\mathcal{O}_I$-factorisation extends along $\mathcal{O}_I \subseteq \mathcal{O}_J$ with the same reduction; and membership and the value of $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}$ are independent of the level, $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(J) = \mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)$ on $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I)$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\sigma$ in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, every natural number $u$ with $u \equiv 1 \pmod{(Mp)/p}$ such that $\sigma\zeta' = \zeta'^{\,u}$ for every $\zeta' \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta'^{\,p} = 1$, and every two subgroups $I$ and $I'$ of $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ that are contained in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, act trivially on the $p$-th roots of unity, and have finite index in that inertia subgroup: for every $y$ in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) lying in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I)$ and such that $\langle u\rangle(\sigma \cdot y) =$ [`ModularCurve.diamondOneBar (M * p) u (σ • y)`](def/ModularCurve_X1Diamond.html#L98) lies in $\mathrm{dom}\mathcal{D}(I')$, the second components of the images under [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.toPic0Pair nodesIg`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L229) of $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I')\bigl(\langle u\rangle(\sigma\cdot y)\bigr)$ and of $\mathrm{sp}\mathcal{D}(I)(y)$ coincide in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the Igusa function field. That is, the diamond-twisted inertia element acts trivially on the second of the two $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-components of the specialisation of a norm-free point.
--
--   This is the inertia-with-diamond clause of the package describing the specialisation of the norm-free part of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$ in terms of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the two Igusa components of the special fibre: after twisting the inertia action by the diamond operator $\langle u \rangle$ matching its action on $\mu_p$, the induced action on the second component of the glued Picard group is trivial. It is used in the assembly of the semistable pinned specialisation family for $J_1(Mp)$ with its Hecke and diamond operators, [`ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_qExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3_family_normFreePart_and_diamond_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div), which in turn feeds the level-lowering step.
